{"name":"Black","hex":"#000000","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Black","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname transferred from the nickname.","A number of places in the United States:\nA town in Geneva County, Alabama.\nAn unincorporated community in Edwards County, Illinois.\nA township in Posey County, Indiana; from the surname.\nAn unincorporated community in Reynolds County, Missouri.\nA township in Somerset County, Pennsylvania; from the surname.\nAn unincorporated community in Mercer County and Wyoming County, West Virginia."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of black (“of or relating to any of various ethnic groups having dark pigmentation of the skin”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Black"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg/500px-Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg/500px-Y